          "type": "turn",
          "question": "What is the difference between flu and a cold?",
          "answer": "**Flu** (influenza) usually starts suddenly. The influenza reference says people can often name the hour it began. Typical symptoms include fever, chills, muscle aches, headache, marked tiredness, and a dry cough. It is also more severe than a cold and can lead to hospital stays, especially in older adults, young children, pregnant people, and people with long-term conditions. (Source: *Influenza*, general clinical reference)\n\n**A cold** (upper respiratory infection) usually starts gradually. It mostly affects the nose and throat, with symptoms like a runny nose and sore throat. High fever or severe exhaustion is uncommon in adults. Most healthy people recover from it on their own. (Source: *Upper respiratory infection (common cold)*, general clinical reference)\n\nThe reference says these differences are useful but not reliable enough to tell which one a person has. Only a clinician or a test can tell them apart with confidence. If you are worried about your own symptoms, or if you have a high fever, trouble breathing, or symptoms that are getting worse, contact a healthcare professional.\n\nThe information I found did not cover treatment, testing, or how long each illness lasts.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
